  <h2 class="!font-serif">What the name means</h2>

  <p>Telfer comes from the Old French <em>taille-fer</em> — from <em>tailler</em>, "to cut", and <em>fer</em>, "iron". Literally, an iron-cleaver. It began as a nickname for a fighting man, and it travelled to Britain with the Normans.</p>

  <p>The name is recorded in Scotland from an early date and is concentrated in the Scottish Borders and Lothian. It is <strong>principally a Scottish surname</strong>, though it also occurs in northern England, where the border ran both ways.</p>

  <h2 class="!font-serif">Telford is not the same name</h2>

  <p>This is the single most common confusion, and it is worth stating plainly: <strong>Telfer and Telford have distinct and unrelated origins.</strong></p>

  <p>Telford <em>looks</em> like a place name — a shallow crossing on a northern river — and there is a town of that name in Shropshire. But the surname as found in northern England is generally understood as a variant of <em>Telfer</em>, re-spelled to fit the familiar place-name pattern. In other words the two names are connected by scribal habit, not by blood.</p>

  <p>If you are researching a Telford and wondering whether they belong in a Telfer tree: the honest answer is that the spelling alone proves nothing, and a documentary link is required.</p>

  <h2 class="!font-serif">Spelling variants you will meet in records</h2>

  <p>Before spelling was standardised, a single family could appear under several forms — sometimes within the same parish register. Variants recorded for this name include:</p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Telfer</strong> — the modern standard form</li>
    <li><strong>Telford</strong> — related by confusion with place names; treat with care</li>
    <li><strong>Telferd</strong>, <strong>Telford</strong>, <strong>Tolfer</strong>, <strong>Talford</strong>, <strong>Telfort</strong> — phonetic and scribal variants found in parish and civil records</li>
  </ul>

  <p>When searching indexes, try the phonetic forms. A clerk writing down a name he had only heard would reach for the nearest familiar spelling — which is exactly why someone searching for their own family today may find nothing under the spelling they expect.</p>

  <h2 class="!font-serif">"Telfer's Cows" and Jamie Telfer of the Fair Dodhead</h2>

  <p>The Telfer name is best known from a Border ballad. <em>Jamie Telfer of the Fair Dodhead</em> was collected by Sir Walter Scott and printed as the seventh ballad in his <em>Minstrelsy of the Scottish Border</em> (1802). It tells of Jamie Telfer, whose cattle were driven off by the Captain of Bewcastle, and who rode for help to recover them.</p>

  <p><strong>It is a ballad, not a record.</strong> Scott himself noted there is no historic precedent for the story, and scholarly commentary on the ballad is explicit that no individual of the name appears in historical documents — Jamie Telfer stands as a representative of the ordinary Border people who were victims of reiving, not as a documented person.</p>

  <p>So this wiki makes no claim that Jamie Telfer was an ancestor. The ballad is preserved here because it is part of the cultural inheritance of the name, and because it is a very good song. You can read the full text and hear Andrew Calhoun's recording on the <a href="/ballads/telfers-cows/">Telfer's Cows</a> page.</p>

  <h2 class="!font-serif">The documented Telfers of this tree</h2>

  <p>The Telfers in this family history are documented in Roxburghshire, in the Scottish Borders, from at least the early eighteenth century. The places that recur in the records are <strong>Castleton</strong> — in Liddesdale, the valley of the Liddel Water — and <strong>Sorbietrees</strong>, a farm name still in use in the same parish.</p>

  <p>Among the earliest individuals recorded here:</p>

  <ul>
    <li><a href={`${import.meta.env.BASE_URL}people/john-telfer-of-castleton-1731/`}>John Telfer of Castleton</a> — born in the Castleton area in the early 1700s</li>
    <li><a href={`${import.meta.env.BASE_URL}people/james-telfer-of-sorbietrees-1740/`}>James Telfer of Sorbietrees</a> — born around 1740, lived his life in the Border country near Newcastleton</li>
    <li><a href={`${import.meta.env.BASE_URL}people/james-telfer-1761/`}>James Telfer</a> — baptised at Castleton on 14 July 1761, died 19 December 1845</li>
    <li><a href={`${import.meta.env.BASE_URL}people/francis-telfer-1768/`}>Francis Telfer</a> — born around 1768 at Sorbietrees</li>
  </ul>

  <p>From there the line can be followed forward through the <a href="/tree/">family tree</a> to the Telfers who emigrated to South Australia.</p>

  <h2 class="!font-serif">Which branch are you?</h2>

  <p>If you have found a Telfer in your own research and want to know whether they connect to this tree, the useful things to establish are: the <strong>parish</strong> of birth or baptism, the <strong>names of both parents</strong>, and the <strong>occupation</strong> recorded for the father. Two men of the same name in the same county is the ordinary situation, not the exception — and a shared name is not evidence of a shared family.</p>

  <p>This wiki names the sources behind every claim on a person's page. Where something is not known, the page says so rather than guessing. That standard is deliberate: a family history is only worth what can be cited.</p>

